  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nThe 19Z water vapor imagery showed several waves over North America,\nwith the forecast area mainly under the influence of a shortwave \nridge. Surface obs showed high pressure centered over the middle MO \nriver valley. Clouds within the surface ridge have struggled to mix \nout causing temps to hang out in the 40s.\n\nFor tonight through Saturday, the forecast area is expected to \nremain between the northern stream and the track of storm systems to \nthe north, while an upper low over AZ cuts off from the mean flow \nand remains south of the forecast area. So with no prospects for \nsynoptic scale forcing, attention turns to the surface high and it's \nimpacts to the sensible weather. First is the cloud cover. The \nlatest RAP and RRFS continue to show a pretty good inversion \nremaining over the cool low level air and inhibiting mixing of the \nboundary layer. This should keep the stratus over the area and \nperhaps have it fill back in to the west this evening. There is a \nbit of a concern for some fog development across parts of north \ncentral KS where the clouds have managed to mix out. The last couple \ndays have seen an axis of dense fog on the western edge of the \nsurface ridge. If the stratus doesn't fill back in, am concerned \nradiational cooling will cause dense fog. For now have leaned \ntowards the low clouds filling back in, but have also added a \nmention of patchy fog for the north central counties. \n\nFor Saturday the surface ridge should begin to break down and allow \nsurface winds to become a little more southwesterly. This is \nexpected to facilitate better mixing and erosion of the inversion \nand low clouds by the afternoon. With this highs should warm to \naround 60. There may be another chance for some patchy fog Saturday \nnight into Sunday with skies clearing out. There may be a little \nmore wind in the boundary layer but the RAP and RRFS both show \nsurface RH values near 100 percent. So have included some patchy fog \nin the forecast, especially for the low lying areas that tend to \ndecouple more easily. \n\nThe mean westerlies look to remain north of the forecast area for \nSunday through Tuesday while the southern stream remains south of \nthe area. With limited moisture and no obvious moisture advection, \nthe forecast is for dry weather to continue with temps gradually \ntrend warmer. The main question with the forecast is how the pattern \nchanges mid to late week. Ensembles show increasing spread in \npotential outcomes by Thursday as the models struggle with an \namplifying upper ridge and trough. According to the 00Z ensembles, \nroughly 25 percent of the runs supported a stronger more amplified \nupper trough over the Midwest that would support the 12Z GFS \nsolution. So the NBM and forecast show moderate temps through the \nend of the workweek. However it is hard to ignore the 12Z ECMWF is \ntrending more in line with the GFS. Even the 12Z AI-GFS is similar \nto the GFS and ECMWF with a closed upper low developing over the \nOhio river valley with meridional flow setting up over the Great \nPlains. If this evening's guidance continues these trends, the \nforecast may be a little too warm for Thursday and Friday of next \nweek.",
